k': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-03T22:09:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ae7bc1e-b222-5460-ae84-48224bb08fc3', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ae7bc1e-b222-5460-ae84-48224bb08fc3'}, {'abstract': 'Brown’s latest novel features a brilliant futurist and a plot that revolves around the tensions between creationism and science.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/03/books/review-origin-dan-brown.html', 'snippet': 'Brown’s latest novel features a brilliant futurist and a plot that revolves around the tensions between creationism and science.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dan Brown has thrown off the doldrums of “Inferno” with a brisk new book that pits creationism against science, and is liable to stir up as much controversy as “The Da Vinci Code” did. In “Origin,” the brash futurist Edmond Kirsch comes up with a theory so bold, so daring that, as he modestly thinks to himself in Brown’s beloved italics, “It will not shake your foundations. It will shatter them.” Kirsch is of course addressing The World, because that’s the scale on which Brown writes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05bookbrown1/05bookbrown1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05bookbrown1/05bookbrown1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05bookbrown1/05bookbrown1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05bookbrown1/05bookbrown1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 488, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05bookbrown1/05bookbrown1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 488, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Dan Brown’s ‘Origin,’ Robert Langdon Returns, With an A.I. Friend in Tow', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Atlas, Buckling a Little', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Origin (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Dan (1964- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-03T22:13:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 