deshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/dfa2b24b-23da-50c7-8905-568def972b1f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/dfa2b24b-23da-50c7-8905-568def972b1f'}, {'abstract': 'In “A Letter to My Nephew” at BAM, Mr. Jones mixes dance and theater to comment on his nephew’s life and on the present moment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/04/arts/dance/review-bill-t-jones-a-letter-to-my-nephew.html', 'snippet': 'In “A Letter to My Nephew” at BAM, Mr. Jones mixes dance and theater to comment on his nephew’s life and on the present moment.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Letter to My Nephew” is what James Baldwin called the first part of his enduringly urgent essay “The Fire Next Time.” A newish work by the Bill T. Jones/Arnie Zane Company carries the similar title “A Letter to My Nephew.” In a program note for the piece, which had its New York debut at the Brooklyn Academy of Music on Tuesday, Mr. Jones calls it “a kind of postcard from uncle to nephew,” and that description seems more accurate. Despite its aspiration to topical relevance and the trendy allusion to Baldwin, this collage of dance theater is as thin as most postcards, and as banal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05BILLTJONES1/05BILLTJONES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2046, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: A Message-Heavy Bill T. Jones Dance-Theater Collage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jones, Bill T/Arnie Zane Dance Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Bill T', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wong, Janet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Briggs, Lance', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Letter to My Nephew (Dance)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-04T16:45:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/293abd36-1b77-523d-96db-45cd19c32e42', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/293abd36-1b77-523d-96db-45cd19c32e42'}, {'abstract': 'Endogenous retroviruses wormed into the human genome eons ago. Today viral genes continue to produce a variety of mysterious proteins in the body.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/04/science/ancient-viruses-dna-genome.html', 'snippet': 'Endogenous retroviruses wormed into the human genome eons ago. Today viral genes continue to produce a variety of mysterious proteins in the body.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In July, scientists reported that a strange protein courses through the veins of pregnant women. No one is sure what it’s there for.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1455, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/science/10SCI-ZIMMER/10SCI-ZIMMER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ancient Viruses Are Buried in Your DNA', 'kicker': 'Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ancient Viruses Wormed Into Our DNA Eons Ago', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Proteins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Viruses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mammals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-04T16:45:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee362f4c-1c00-554b-8c4b-1a0e5a26b8b7', 'word_count': 1180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee362f4c-1c00-554b-8c4b-1a0e5a26b8b7'}, {'abstract': 'The singer, who was part of a wave known as future R&B, isn’t ready to move from the fringes to the mainstream on her debut album.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/04/arts/music/kelela-take-me-apart-review.html', 'snippet': 'The singer, who was part of a wave known as future R&B, isn’t ready to move from the fringes to the mainstream on her debut album.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Take Me Apart,” the title song from Kelela’s debut album, is a spiraling, labyrinthine seduction. “You don’t know my bed/well tonight you might find out,” she sings quietly. Within moments a muffled beat has quickened into a thudding double time pulse as she commands “Take me apart.” She backs off, “afraid of fallin’,” only to get swept up again. There’s a swerve, abetted by a jazzy close-harmony choir of Kelelas, as she notes she’s been up late writing a song, then gives a mandate — “Don’t say you’re in love/Until you learn to take me apart” — on the way to another burst of double time and a dreamy, satisfied coda.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/arts/05KELELA2/05KELELA2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kelela’s ‘Take Me Apart’ Is R&B That’s Intimate Inside and Out', 'kicker': 'Album Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_head