None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Megan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Specia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c761709c-ff83-54c3-97a9-6800978deda3', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c761709c-ff83-54c3-97a9-6800978deda3'}, {'abstract': 'We talk about the complications of occupying two (or more) racial identities in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/05/podcasts/still-processing-being-biracial.html', 'snippet': 'We talk about the complications of occupying two (or more) racial identities in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For months, the two of us have been trying to figure out a way to have a conversation about the experience of being biracial. This week we just go for it. First, we talk about the cultural and historical suspicion America still has of black-white interracial romantic relationships. It gives us an excuse to revisit the reason “Get Out” has been one of the year’s major movies: It articulates the previously inarticulable about race. Then we consider the offspring of interracial coupling — whether the possibility of occupying two identities (or more) is a choice, a luxury or a delusion; and what fears, doubts or envy nonbiracial black Americans might feel about biracial black Americans. We drop in on Spike Lee’s “School Daze” and the sitcom “Black-ish.” We consider our feelings about Rashida Jones, Drake and Vin Diesel. We unpack the writings of Zadie Smith and Barack Obama. And we kind of have to ask: Aren’t we all a little bit mixed?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/05/magazine/05still-processing-biracial1/05still-processing-biracial1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 't