dbacks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1062, 'width': 1913, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'M.L.B. Investigating Diamondbacks Coach Who Wore Apple Watch', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Arizona Coach’s Apple Watch Scrutinized', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wearable Computing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARIZONA DIAMONDBACKS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-05T23:24:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d61023cb-0634-52a8-aaa0-27611ac6a23d', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d61023cb-0634-52a8-aaa0-27611ac6a23d'}, {'abstract': 'Global capital markets don’t matter than much even in the medium run.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/10/05/the-transfer-problem-and-tax-incidence-insanely-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'Global capital markets don’t matter than much even in the medium run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These days, what passes for policymaking in America manages to be simultaneously farcical and sinister, and the evil-clown aspects extend into the oddest places. Hence the tale of the Mnuchin Treasury’s incompetent attempt to suppress an internal analysis – to send it down the mnemory hole? — on the incidence of corporate taxation. This paper reached the inconvenient conclusion that most of the tax stays where it’s applied – with the owners of corporate capital – with only a small share falling indirectly on workers. In general, attempts to suppress stuff like this fail thanks to leaks. But in this case the paper has already been published in a peer-reviewed journal, so that even if the Treasury were locked down tight everyone could read it anyway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Transfer Problem and Tax Incidence (Insanely Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-10-05T23:25:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43a033ce-00a4-5df9-a358-afc1c03ab34d', 'word_count': 1468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43a033ce-00a4-5df9-a358-afc1c03ab34d'}, {'abstract': 'The Yahoo breach is another reminder that the right to privacy is dying, if not already dead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/05/opinion/privacy-rights-security-breaches.html', 'snippet': 'The Yahoo breach is another reminder that the right to privacy is dying, if not already dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We learned on Tuesday that three billion Yahoo email accounts were compromised in 2013. In early September, it was Equifax’s 143 million credit reports. Just a few months before that, we learned 198 million United States voter records were leaked online in June.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/06/opinion/06BurtGeer/06BurtGeer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard'