, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Armed Services', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-06T23:28:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Gibbons-Neff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gibbons-Neff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66dfd2ee-d9d2-578f-9db9-0376aba01aa6', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66dfd2ee-d9d2-578f-9db9-0376aba01aa6'}, {'abstract': 'The Selective Service wasn’t just a way to get men into the Army during Vietnam. It was a tool for social engineering too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/06/opinion/vietnam-draft.html', 'snippet': 'The Selective Service wasn’t just a way to get men into the Army during Vietnam. It was a tool for social engineering too.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Greeting: You are hereby ordered for induction in the Armed Forces of the United States.” In 1967, more than 300,000 American men opened envelopes with this statement inside. Few pieces of mail ever incited the same combination of panic, anticipation and resignation as a draft notice. The words struck terror in the hearts of many recipients. Others found them comforting after years of waiting for the Selective Service System to come calling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/07/opinion/07vietnamSub/07vietnamSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail