y Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Wehner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wehner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/29f6374a-942f-5556-bcac-a66966dc7eac', 'word_count': 1155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29f6374a-942f-5556-bcac-a66966dc7eac'}, {'abstract': 'Global warming rejecters like President Trump and Scott Pruitt are having more and more difficulty defending their position.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/07/opinion/sunday/hurricanes-climate-public-opinion.html', 'snippet': 'Global warming rejecters like President Trump and Scott Pruitt are having more and more difficulty defending their position.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Climate images have never been able to convey our full planetary danger until now. The extraordinary recent four-punch sequence of hurricanes — Harvey, Irma, Jose and Maria — threatened the lives of millions of people, obliterated their homes and has raised doubts that some places will ever recover. The rest of us have a newly immediate sense of catastrophes of biblical proportions. As meaning-hungry creatures we search for explanations. No wonder some have embraced the apocalyptic narrative of total destruction by an angry deity. And no wonder that climate-change rejecters like President Trump have increasing difficulty defending their position.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 448}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08lifton/08lifton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1530, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Changing Climate Mind-Set', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our Changing Climate Mind-Set', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pruitt, Scott', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-07T18:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Jay Lifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'Jay', 'lastname': 'Lifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/96d0637d-84cd-5d9a-ba29-e533e692aefb', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96d0637d-84cd-5d9a-ba29-e533e692aefb'}, {'abstract': 'A friend, reflecting on his son’s opioid journey, once remarked, “I wish I’d held him closer.” Is it ever close enough?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/07/opinion/sunday/when-an-epidemic-is-personal.html', 'snippet': 'A friend, reflecting on his son’s opioid journey, once remarked, “I wish I’d held him closer.” Is it ever close enough?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s how I imagined it would go over the summer: He would have come up to stay with me and my husband for a long weekend, maybe a week even, away from the friends who gave him shelter when he was using drugs. From the specter that seemed to surface every few months like clockwork. We’d known him since he was in the womb, but that wasn’t the same as knowing him now. Still, we thought he’d come to stay and go to the beach nearby and body surf and stroll along the esplanade, smoking, gazing out at the swans and the geese he loved to feed back when he was a little boy and would come to visit.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/sunday/08chicurel/08chicurel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When an Epidemic Is Personal', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fishing for Hope in the Face of Addiction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Addiction (Psychology)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-07T18:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Chicurel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chicurel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9db01ba3-de48-5c85-a9f4-ee03f7402bd7', 'word_count': 1078, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9db01ba3-de48-5c85-a9f4-ee03f7402bd7'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor de Blasio has failed to make distinctions that should have been apparent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/07/opinion/sunday/robert-e-lee-christopher-columbus-and-petain.html', 'snippet': 'Mayor de Blasio has failed to make distinctions that should have been apparent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill de Blasio, born Warren Wilhelm Jr., has long identified with his Italian heritage, bequeathed by his mother’s family. But to the organizers of a Columbus Day parade that will be held Sunday in the Bronx, the mayor is, as one of them put it, “a fake Italian.” He will not reign on their parade. That’s because he is conspicuously not invited (though he plans to march Monday in the big Columbus Day procession along Fifth Avenue).', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 432}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1473, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun2web/08sun2web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert E. Lee, Christopher Columbus ... and Pétain?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Robert E. Lee, Columbus ... and Pétain?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Columbus, Christopher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petain, Philippe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-07T18:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d958e6e9-4721-5f27-8ab6-d40ebe7772f7', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d958e6e9-4721-5f27-8ab6-d40ebe7772f7'}, {'abstract': 'It’s unsettling to hear ordinary North Koreans talk of war with calm acceptance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/07/opinion/sunday/north-korea-stalemate.html', 'snippet': 'It’s unsettling to hear ordinary North Koreans talk of war with calm acceptance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pyongyang, North Korea — North Korea’s capital city is awash in propaganda. Posters depicting missiles, some striking the United States Capitol, hang along major streets. In recent days, a million civilians, including high school students, factory workers and older men who long ago completed their military service, have signed up at the government’s request to fight the United States, if needed.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/opinion/08sun3web1/08sun3web1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing the North Korean Stalemate From the Other Side', 'kicker': 'Editorial Observer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seeing a Nuclear Stalemate From the Other Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Korean War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-07T18:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Giacomo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giacomo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/de4073ac-5a40-51e3-ab01-c8de72ca01dd', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de4073ac-5a40-51e3-ab01-c8de72ca01dd'}, {'abstract': 'The void is as old as time, but it was a human innovation to harness it with a symbol.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/07/opinion/sunday/who-invented-zero.html', 'snippet': 'The void is as old as time, but it was a human innovation to harness it with a symbol.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carbon dating of an ancient Indian document, the Bakhshali manuscript, has recently placed the first written occurrence of the number zero in the third or fourth century A.D., about 500 years earlier than previously believed. While the news has no practical bearing on the infrastructure of zeros (and ones) underlying our high-tech civilization, it does remind us how indebted we are for this invention. But to whom is this debt owed? And how should it be repaid?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0