 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/09/sports/soccer/world-cup-qatar-2022-al-thawadi.html', 'snippet': 'In an interview with The New York Times, the man leading Qatar’s preparations for the 2022 World Cup discussed the advantages, and challenges, of the task he was given.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Qatar won the right to host the 2022 World Cup seven years ago, and it still has five years to get its country ready to roll out the welcome mat. But as world soccer begins to focus more intently on the 2018 tournament in Russia this week, and the teams that will be taking part, thoughts of 2022 are never far away. Hassan al-Thawadi, the British-educated son of a diplomat leading the 2022 World Cup organizing committee, is the man tasked with putting on his country’s big show. In an interview at The New York Times last month, al-Thawadi revealed that he supports — gasp! — Lyon and not Qatari-owned Paris St.-Germain. He also discussed worker welfare, the unique challenges of hosting a World Cup in a Muslim nation, and the World Cup stadium that will have a second life as a wedding hall.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/10/sports/soccer/10qatarQandA1/merlin-to-scoop-127725686-150093-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hassan al-Thawadi on Worker Welfare, Qatari Goals and the 2022 World Cup', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man in Charge Discusses Qatar’s Plans for the 2022 World Cup', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'al-Thawadi, Hassan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2022 (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-09T13:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by The New York Times', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/669eed36-5de9-5889-9f43-5c3b18f4f7ca', 'word_count': 2242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/669eed36-5de9-5889-9f43-5c3b18f4f7ca'}, {'abstract': 'Hopefully, his new documentary will jump-start a new era of interest and scholarship around the Vietnam War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/09/opinion/ken-burns-vietnam.html', 'snippet': 'Hopefully, his new documentary will jump-start a new era of interest and scholarship around the Vietnam War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With any luck, the television documentary “The Vietnam War” by Ken Burns and Lynn Novick may open the way for a new period of public and academic interest in the conflict. That may sound strange: How many movies, books, poems and documentaries have been made about the war, after all? A lot, of course. But with few exceptions, the “conversation” around the war has been stuck in neutral, focused on a few important but far from all-encompassing questions. But the Burns-Novick documentary could change that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/09/opinion/09vietnam-weistWeb/09vietnam-weistWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/09/opinion/09vietnam-weistWeb/09vietnam-weistWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/09/opinion/09vietnam-weistWeb/09vietnam-weistWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/09/opinion/09vietnam-weistWeb/09vietnam-weistWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag