nsurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-10T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Reader Center', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb3c24bb-03b6-53cb-84fd-2177491a9c4b', 'word_count': 4313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb3c24bb-03b6-53cb-84fd-2177491a9c4b'}, {'abstract': 'From a tiny village in the country’s rural east, a leading nationalist intellectual builds a vision for the future of his movement across Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/10/magazine/the-prophet-of-germanys-new-right.html', 'snippet': 'From a tiny village in the country’s rural east, a leading nationalist intellectual builds a vision for the future of his movement across Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Götz Kubitschek, a self-proclaimed “rightist intellectual,” lives in a medieval manor house in Schnellroda, a rural village in eastern Germany. From this isolated, antique outpost, Kubitschek, who is 47, wields considerable influence over far-right thinkers, activists and politicians across Germany, who make regular pilgrimages to Schnellroda for an audience with him. The manor serves as the headquarters for the magazine and publishing house that Kubitschek runs with his wife, the writer Ellen Kositza, and also for a rightist think tank, the plainly named Institute for State Policy, and a small organic farm where he raises rabbits and goats. Kubitschek calls himself a conservative, battling to preserve Germany’s “ethno-cultural identity,” which he says is threatened by immigration and the alienating effects of modernity. He identifies as part of the German “New Right,” which seeks to dissociate itself from the “old right,” which in Germany means Nazis. German political scientists, by contrast, classify the brand of thinking Kubitschek ascribes to as either an ideological “hinge” between conservatism and right-wing extremism, or as simply extremist — not vastly different, in other words, from the old right. Kubitschek, however, presents his views with a disarming, Teutonic idealism that recalls a Germany that long preceded the rise of Hitler. The German magazine Der Spiegel once referred to him as a “dark knight.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15germany1/15germany1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Prophet of Germany’s New Right', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Prophet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kubitschek, Gotz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pegida (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alternative for Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hocke, Bjorn (1972- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Neo Nazi Groups', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-10T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Angelos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Angelos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec1b5f1d-d097-545b-bc6f-1a5addbf68a1', 'word_count': 5811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec1b5f1d-d097-545b-bc6f-1a5addbf68a1'}, {'abstract': 'The author and humorist behind the best-selling “The Rap Year Book’’ takes on basketball in an illustrated book that asks and answers a lot of questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/10/sports/basketball/shea-serrano-basketball.html', 'snippet': 'The author and humorist behind the best-selling “The Rap Year Book’’ takes on basketball in an illustrated book that asks and answers a lot of questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shea Serrano, the sportswriter and the San Antonio Spurs’ virtual cheerleader, made a name for himself by finding humor in the mundane on his Twitter feed. He has 169,000 followers and climbing. But what really brought him fame was the 2015 best-selling book “The Rap Year Book,” a laugh-out-loud, year-by-year argument for the best songs in hip-hop. Now, he is back with another eagerly anticipated, illustrated book, “Basketball (and Other Things): A Collection of Questions Asked, Answered, Illustrated,” which will be published Tuesday. Sample question: “If 1997 Karl Malone and a bear swapped places for a season, who would be more successful?”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/10serrano-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 442, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/10serrano-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 442}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/10serrano-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 755, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/10serrano-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1510, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/11serrano-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/11serrano-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/sports/11serrano/11serrano-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150,