timedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/6a68c214-2927-5711-a768-d1f382561f28', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/6a68c214-2927-5711-a768-d1f382561f28'}, {'abstract': 'At Frieze London, Alexander Coggin focuses on attendants’ clavicles to reveal clues about their roles in the art world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/10/t-magazine/art/frieze-london-photos-alexander-coggin.html', 'snippet': 'At Frieze London, Alexander Coggin focuses on attendants’ clavicles to reveal clues about their roles in the art world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At this year’s Frieze Art Fair in London, which wrapped up on Sunday, the photographer Alexander Coggin assumed the role of an art-world anthropologist for an ongoing series of what he terms “clavicle studies.” As Coggin says: “An event like Frieze comes with a coded social hierarchy courting many archetypes: the Gallerist, the Artist, the Curator, the Collector, the Critic. Every tribe ascribes to a different dress code according to their position in the art/business paradigm.” By focusing only on the subject’s clavicle area, Coggin assembles a vivid collection of character clues, allowing the viewer’s imagination to fill in the rest.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/09/t-magazine/09tmag-clavicle-slide-03V1/09tmag-clavicle-slide-03V1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/09/t-magazine/09tmag-clavicle-slide-03V1/09tmag-clavicle-slide-03V1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/09/t-magazine/09t