 514, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/07/insider/07missingobjects/07missingobjects-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 514, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Teaching Activities for: ‘Why We Miss Objects That Are Right in Front of Us’', 'kicker': 'Article of the Day', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-10-11T08:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Proulx', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Proulx', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/449e1b71-e22d-53c1-810b-69c563e77e61', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/449e1b71-e22d-53c1-810b-69c563e77e61'}, {'abstract': 'For Republicans, the purges never stop, and the leadership void is never filled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/11/opinion/steve-bannon-revolution.html', 'snippet': 'For Republicans, the purges never stop, and the leadership void is never filled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The only fitting reaction to the news that Steve Bannon intends to support primary challengers against Republican incumbents from sea to shining sea is a terrible, almost Teutonic sort of world-weariness. Bannon’s grand ambitions should inspire the same soul-deadening déjà vu, the existential exhaustion, with which Bill Murray’s weatherman greeted every morning in Punxsutawney, Penn. They should bring to mind both Friedrich Nietzsche’s idea of eternal recurrence and his warning that if you stare deep into the abyss, it stares into you. They should inspire a vision of the Republican Party as a wheel turning endlessly in darkness, with the illusion of movement but the light forever out of reach.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1338, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/11/opinion/11douthatWeb/11douthatWeb-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bannon Revolution', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bannon, Stephen K', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libertarianism (US Politics)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-11T08:26:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/23eaefdd-bc6c-5b04-a8de-a7ff31eb12aa', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23eaefdd-bc6c-5b04-a8de-a7ff31eb12aa'}, {'abstract': 'Lee Friedlander provided an early study of our national fascination with statuary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/11/magazine/the-photographer-who-saw-americas-monuments-hiding-in-plain-sight.html', 'snippet': 'Lee Friedlander provided an early study of our national fascination with statuary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lee Friedlander’s “The American Monument” was first published in 1976. That’s “monument” singular, though one of the many singular things about Friedlander is that he’s nothing if not a pluralist. Whitman-like, he is great, contains multitudes. In an essay appended to the sumptuous new edition of this landmark work, Peter Galassi (who curated the 2005 Friedlander retrospective at the Museum of Modern Art) deems it “pointless” to try to count precisely how many books the photographer has published since 1976 before settling on roughly one a year. The retrospective was huge, and, inevitably, the accompanying catalog was almost too hefty to lug home comfortably. It was sort of monumental, though monuments tend to be erected to the dead.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15mag-15onphoto-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15mag-15onphoto-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15mag-15onphoto-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15mag-15onphoto-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1339, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15onphoto1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15onphoto1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15onphoto1-copy/15onphoto1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Photographer Who Saw America’s Monuments Hiding in Plain Sight', 'kicker': 'On Photography', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Photographer Who Saw America’s Monuments Hiding in Plain Sight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Center of Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedlander, Lee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-11T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Dyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18bb14f4-1ebd-55bf-987c-e7d4d1b9f838', 'word_count': 1456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18bb14f4-1ebd-55bf-987c-e7d4d1b9f838'}, {'abstract': 'The podcaster on not calling people “trolls,” being combative on the internet and why he genuinely loves talking to people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/11/magazine/dylan-marron-wants-to-know-why-people-hate-one-another.html', 'snippet': 'The podcaster on not calling people “trolls,” being combative on the internet and why he genuinely loves talking to people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your podcast, “Conversations With People Who Hate Me,” is exactly what it sounds like: You interview people who have posted racist, homophobic or otherwise terrible things about you on the internet. Fast Company said it’s the “timeliest podcast of 2017.” Why would you want to put yourself through this? Just because someone says something outrageously mean to you online doesn’t mean they’re sad and lonely. I wanted to go toward these messages rather than run away.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/magazine/15talk-promo/