 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mountain Lions and Cougars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science Advances (Journal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elbroch, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-11T18:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41c48919-ec4f-5493-bac7-5cf92db80674', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41c48919-ec4f-5493-bac7-5cf92db80674'}, {'abstract': 'The 22 major blazes raging in California have been much more ferocious than the state’s typical fire season, for reasons that were years in the making.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/11/us/california-fires-questions.html', 'snippet': 'The 22 major blazes raging in California have been much more ferocious than the state’s typical fire season, for reasons that were years in the making.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Intense, fast-moving fires have been raging across much of California since Sunday night. The blazes have barreled through communities like freight trains, turning homes to dust in a blink and leaving at least 23 people dead. The largest of the fires are in the state’s wine country  north of San Francisco.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/12/us/12firequestions-01/12firequestions-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/12/us/12firequestions-01/1