d Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-16T01:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Columnists', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8388a247-2e39-5812-a0f1-fc7b80001039', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8388a247-2e39-5812-a0f1-fc7b80001039'}, {'abstract': 'Saru finds himself in command of the Discovery, while we learn something rather explosive about Lorca.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/15/arts/television/star-trek-discovery-season-1-episode-5-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Saru finds himself in command of the Discovery, while we learn something rather explosive about Lorca.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the best episodes of “Star Trek: The Next Generation” was the two-part “The Best of Both Worlds,” in which the Borg kidnap Picard and turn him into Locutus. Through a different lens, though, this was a story about Riker (Jonathan Frakes), who is forced to take command of the Enterprise and is unsure whether he is ready to do so. On its surface, the story was about the lengths a crew will go to save Picard and the potential destruction of the Federation. But it was also, if not more so, about seeing Riker develop beyond just a competent first officer. (Part of this was due to Patrick Stewart’s contract negotiations.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/16/arts/television/16trekrecap/16trekrecap-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Star Trek: Discovery’ Season 1, Episode 5: Saru’s Moment', 'kicker': 'Star Trek: Discovery', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Star Trek: Discovery (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Rainn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-16T01:45:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sopan Deb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sopan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Deb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9577eb16-b397-50be-9cd4-4e82699a01f2', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9577eb16-b397-50be-9cd4-4e82699a01f2'}, {'abstract': 'Sisyphus will continue to get paid for rolling that boulder up the hill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/15/arts/television/the-deuce-recap-why-me.html', 'snippet': 'Sisyphus will continue to get paid for rolling that boulder up the hill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the third season of David Simon’s “The Wire,” a rogue Baltimore police major named “Bunny” Colvin experiments with a project called “Hamsterdam,” a three-zone area where drug dealers and users can congregate and do their business without getting harassed by the cops. Nearing the end of his career, Colvin puts legalization in the petri dish, theorizing that Hamsterdam will lessen crimes in afflicted neighborhoods and allow social services to distribute condoms and clean needles in a concentrated space. Although the show is careful to show the deplorable state of these fetid, smoke-filled rowhouses, the stats wind up supporting his thesis. As a reward, he’s hustled to early retirement and the streets return to the status quo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/15/watching/15the-deuce/15the-deuce-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/15/watching/15the-deuce/15the-deuce-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xl