1:31:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c797c743-e6bd-5fa7-8f37-26bc89f3d370', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c797c743-e6bd-5fa7-8f37-26bc89f3d370'}, {'abstract': 'This strong Mexican spirit from Michoacan is buried underground in a cemetery to age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/16/dining/mezcal-cemetery-mexico.html', 'snippet': 'This strong Mexican spirit from Michoacan is buried underground in a cemetery to age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a tradition in Mexico — especially in the State of Michoacan, west of Mexico City — to bury special batches of mezcal in large glass bottles to age, often in cemeteries. Don Lupe Perez, the distiller at Mezcales de Leyenda, has released a limited edition of just such a mezcal. The clear, slightly viscous liquor is smoky, with rich, fruity notes. It’s a spirit for sipping, perhaps with a simple squirt of lime and a salty nibble alongside: Mezcales de Leyenda, Cementerio Mezcalero, $269.96, Astor Wine & Spirits, 399 Lafayette Street (East Fourth Street), 212-674-7500, astorwines.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/18/dining/18burnermezcal/18burnermezcal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 816, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/18/dining/18burnermezcal/18burnermezcal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 816}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/1