ng Norman Bates was shot over seven days in 1959, and every element is instantly recognizable: the shadowy figure tearing aside the shower curtain, the stream of blood and water circling the drain, Bernard Herrmann’s shrieking violins. The scene has been dissected by scholars and critics and parodied by everyone from Mel Brooks to “The Simpsons”; its score has become aural shorthand for “something really scary is about to happen.” Is there anything a “Psycho” fan still might not know about this most famous of cinematic moments?', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/22/arts/22PSYCHO-SCENE6/22PSYCHO-SCENE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/22/arts/22PSYCHO-SCENE6/22PSYCHO-SCENE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/22/arts/22PSYCHO-SCENE6/22PSYCHO-SCENE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 540, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/22/arts/22PSYCHO-SCENE6/22PSYCHO-SCENE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/22/arts/22PSYCHO-SCENE6/22PSYCHO-SCENE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/22/arts/22PSYCHO-SCENE6/22PSYCHO-SCENE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'