'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zatz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/352f1e68-d60f-53c8-af8b-4b495d607c31', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/352f1e68-d60f-53c8-af8b-4b495d607c31'}, {'abstract': 'Goldman Sachs posted strong results, but its rival on Wall Street has earnings that look more repeatable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/17/business/dealbook/goldman-sachs-morgan-stanley-earnings.html', 'snippet': 'Goldman Sachs posted strong results, but its rival on Wall Street has earnings that look more repeatable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Goldman Sachs edged past Morgan Stanley on style points in the third quarter, but not on substance. Lloyd Blankfein’s firm cranked out $2 billion of earnings in the three months to the end of September, at an annualized return on equity of 10.9 percent. That bested the 9.6 percent showing of Goldman’s main Wall Street rival — the first time this year that it has come out ahead. Morgan Stanley’s boss, James Gorman, though, has crafted a more attractive-looking business for the longer term.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman Sachs Had Good Earnings, but It Could Learn From Morgan Stanley', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-17T19:15:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Antony Currie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Currie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c95c0b69-9164-5f2c-993f-2733c71c540d', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c95c0b69-9164-5f2c-993f-2733c71c540d'}, {'abstract': 'In “Generation Wealth,” the photographer shows that she can satirize her subjects. But does she give insight into why we live the way we do?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/17/arts/design/review-laura-greenfield-generation-wealth-international-center-photography-icp.html', 'snippet': 'In “Generation Wealth,” the photographer shows that she can satirize her subjects. But does she give insight into why we live the way we do?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are some things money can’t buy: for example, intimacy. Few people, rich or poor, really like to disclose their financial standing to the world — which means a photographer interested in wealth and poverty needs a special gift for seduction. Whether depicting privation, as in Gordon Parks’s indelible essay of a struggling Harlem family, or privilege, as in the high society portraits of Tina Barney, the most powerful images of American money rely on trust. They are mutual exchanges between lenser and lensed, although there is always a third element in the frame: the economy itself.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/20/arts/design/20greenfield1/06greenfield1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/20/arts/design/20greenfield1/06greenfield1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/20/arts/design/20greenfield1/06greenfield1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, '