to cultural events in New York City for families with children and teenagers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/19/arts/events-for-children-in-nyc-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Our guide to cultural events in New York City for families with children and teenagers.', 'lead_paragraph': '‘A BOOK AS A BRIDGE AMONG TIME AND SPACE’ at the Children’s Museum of the Arts (Oct. 22, 2:45 p.m.). Many artists make statements with brushes and pens; Elena Berriolo uses a needle. Equipped with a sewing machine, she stitches books in a process that is as important as the product. On Sunday she will invite families visiting the museum — they will meet in the lobby — to join her on the No. 1 train from 3 to 5:30 p.m., as she makes a round-trip journey from Houston Street in Manhattan to Van Cortlandt Park in the Bronx, all the while sewing a 40-foot-long accordion-style book; passengers will help hold the pages. By highlighting more than the geographic distance between the two neighborhoods — the average life span of a child born in Greenwich Village is greater than that of one born anywhere in the Bronx — Ms. Berriolo intends to protest efforts to repeal the Affordable Care Act. Her performance is part of “In Practice: Works in Progress,” a show through Oct. 29 at the museum, where five professional artists have set up studios to create projects alongside young visitors. 212-274-0986, cmany.org', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/20/arts/20listings-kids/20listings-kids-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/20/arts/20listings-kids/20listings-kids-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/20/arts/20