: 'Central America', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-20T08:55:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Goldberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9ba0d22-1d2a-561f-8065-42b81b48687d', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9ba0d22-1d2a-561f-8065-42b81b48687d'}, {'abstract': 'Cities used to be tied to geography: a river, a port, the lee side of a mountain range.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/20/opinion/how-amazon-took-seattles-soul.html', 'snippet': 'Cities used to be tied to geography: a river, a port, the lee side of a mountain range.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — I live in the city that hit the Amazon jackpot, now the biggest company town in America. Long before the mad dash to land the second headquarters for the world’s largest online retailer, Amazon found us. Since then, we’ve been overwhelmed by a future we never had any say over.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/20/opinion/20eganWeb/20eganWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/20/opinion/20eganWeb/20eganWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/20/opinion/20eganWeb/20eganWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo'