g the protective ozone layer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert L. de Zafra, a physicist who helped confirm that the chemicals in some aerosols and refrigerants were responsible for the expanding ozone hole over Antarctica, died on Oct. 10 in Stony Brook, N.Y. He was 85.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 821}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 749, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1043, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/23/world/dezafra-obit2/dezafra-obit2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert de Zafra, Who Made Key Findings on Ozone, Dies at 85', 'kicker': None, 'content_kick