value': 'Costumes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-23T19:43:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Lombard and Bonnie Wertheim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lombard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bonnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wertheim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bec8831-e2da-5432-a2c0-a6d063864cb6', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bec8831-e2da-5432-a2c0-a6d063864cb6'}, {'abstract': 'The seedy gluten-free crumble from the popular San Francisco restaurant doubles as a topping for salads and yogurt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/23/dining/state-bird-seed-snack.html', 'snippet': 'The seedy gluten-free crumble from the popular San Francisco restaurant doubles as a topping for salads and yogurt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Render is a new company that collaborates with chefs on food products. Its first, State Bird Seed, is a crumble made with the guidance of Stuart Brioza and Nicole Krasinski, the chefs and owners of State Bird Provisions in San Francisco. A mixture of seeds with puffed quinoa and seasonings comes in small pieces to use as toppings for salads, yogurt, eggs, pasta or anything that needs some crunch. It goes without saying that the cook is likely to succumb to snacking along the way. There is a sea salt variety, one with almonds and rosemary, and another with seaweed: State Bird Seed, $28 for six 3.5-ounce packages, renderfood.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/dining/25burnersnack/25burnersnack-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400,