'By Jean Yves Chainon and Tim Chaffee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': 'Yves', 'lastname': 'Chainon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chaffee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/88e4191e-1f67-598f-8c31-c233a98ced27', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/88e4191e-1f67-598f-8c31-c233a98ced27'}, {'abstract': 'While President Trump contemplates the relationship between the United States and Cuba, more museums are attracting crowds fascinated by Cuban art and culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/24/arts/cuba-art-politics.html', 'snippet': 'While President Trump contemplates the relationship between the United States and Cuba, more museums are attracting crowds fascinated by Cuban art and culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cuba is like a giant Ping-Pong, caught in the cross hairs of United States foreign policy. One minute President Obama is relaxing restrictions, making it easier for tourists in the United States to visit the tiny island. Blink, and President Trump is rolling back some of the administration’s changes. Another blink, and the state department is advising Americans not to travel to Cuba after mysterious medical attacks on diplomats at the American Embassy in Havana.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/29/arts/29CUBA1/29CUBA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/29/arts/29CUBA1/29CUBA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/1