tro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles V. Bagli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Bagli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49dd52c1-4063-54cf-a92f-674c5d6ad9f3', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49dd52c1-4063-54cf-a92f-674c5d6ad9f3'}, {'abstract': 'My family’s accomplishments have not shielded us from the indiscriminate damage of President Trump’s travel ban.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/25/opinion/prosecution-terrorists-bush-iran.html', 'snippet': 'My family’s accomplishments have not shielded us from the indiscriminate damage of President Trump’s travel ban.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. LOUIS — In 1977, when I was 2 years old, my family and I moved from Iran to America so that my father could pursue his doctorate in chemistry at Michigan State University. Two years later, Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini seized power and Iran was no longer the country my family knew. We stayed in the United States, becoming permanent residents and eventually citizens by 1999. My parents were raised Muslim but they stopped practicing after leaving Iran, so our household was secular.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25khazaeliWeb/25khazaeliWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25khazaeliWeb/25khazaeliWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 440}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25khazaeliWeb/25khazaeliWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumb