al institutions, ire against banks was reignited.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Banks just scored a victory against disgruntled customers — and, in the longer term, against themselves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Count on a Political Backlash to the Banks’ Victory in the Senate', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-25T20:32:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gina Chon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec4cb4c4-416d-56b6-b0c8-6b8a4c4610bd', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec4cb4c4-416d-56b6-b0c8-6b8a4c4610bd'}, {'abstract': 'Calls to remove works from museums because of racism and animal cruelty are an opportunity, not just censorship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/25/opinion/guggenheim-artwork-animals-racism.html', 'snippet': 'Calls to remove works from museums because of racism and animal cruelty are an opportunity, not just censorship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Under pressure from online petitions and on-site protests, the Guggenheim Museum last month withdrew or modified three works of art that involved animals from its survey of Chinese contemporary art, called “Art and China After 1989: Theater of the World.” The titular work in the show, a sculptural vitrine, was originally filled with live insects and reptiles, which the museum removed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1339, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/opinion/25borgonjonWeb/25borgonjonWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Destroying an Artwork', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guggenheim, Solomon R, Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walker Art Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Museum of American Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-25T20:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Xu Borgonjon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'Xu', 'lastname': 'Borgonjon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c22c933-c505-5ca7-b2b4-bdfddb5d04c1', 'word_count': 1051, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c22c933-c505-5ca7-b2b4-bdfddb5d04c1'}, {'abstract': 'Is the West trapped in a demographic-political doom loop?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/10/25/liberalism-and-the-politics-of-low-fertility/', 'snippet': 'Is the West trapped in a demographic-political doom loop?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Atlantic’s Derek Thompson has a very interesting piece this week on what he calls the “doom loop” of Western liberalism\xa0—\xa0in which low birth rates slow growth and create a pressing need for new immigrants, which in turn feeds xenophobia and leads to a\xa0decline in support for the welfare state, which leads to stratification, further discontent and an authoritarian turn, which presumably slows growth further, etc., etc., until liberalism goes kaput. It’s an argument that dovetails with my own fertility-related preoccupations, but not surprisingly I think it’s limited somewhat by Thompson’s\xa0own assumptions, which I’ll push back against gently here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Liberalism and the Politics of Low Fertility', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Rates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Workers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liberalism (US Politics)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merkel, Angela', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-25T20:43:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/494388e6-625f-51b1-889c-e8d1d129f547', 'word_count': 1079, 'uri': 'nyt://article/494388e6-625f-51b1-889c-e8d1d129f547'}, {'abstract': 'Like the “cram schools” of Asia, academies prepare students — many from immigrant families — for everything from A.P. exams to New York’s high school admissions test.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/25/magazine/asian-test-prep-centers-offer-parents-exactly-what-they-want-results.html', 'snippet': 'Like the “cram schools” of Asia, academies prepare students — many from immigrant families — for everything from A.P. exams to New York’s high school admissions test.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Main Street in Flushing, Queens’s Chinatown, the GPS Academy building announces itself with a sign printed in both English and Chinese, hanging over a crush of bubble-tea shops and souvenir street vendors. Inside, exactly 10 felt pennants adorn the back wall of the main office, bearing the titles “Harvard,” “Yale,” “Princeton” and so on. They seem to wield a hushed influence over the academy’s students, daring them to imagine one day being accepted to such universities. But it’s unlikely that they really need the reminder. They’ve known those names for a long while.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1410, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/25/magazine/25mag-testprep/25mag-testprep-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge