nt_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Innovation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALEXANDER, LAMAR', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Patty', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-27T08:51:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lanhee J. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lanhee', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7ada520-1ea4-51c5-b0dc-256af0abf93c', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7ada520-1ea4-51c5-b0dc-256af0abf93c'}, {'abstract': 'I was interrogated without explanation, in a country that often confuses racial profiling with improving national security.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/27/opinion/danger-racial-profiling-america.html', 'snippet': 'I was interrogated without explanation, in a country that often confuses racial profiling with improving national security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'San Francisco — Every day, American citizens are detained in windowless rooms at international airports with little to no scrutiny. I am not referring to airports in some foreign country; I am referring to our airports here in the United States. I know, because it happened to me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27bastian-1/27bastian-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Danger in a Face', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Racial Profiling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARMENIA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-27T08:51:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Bastian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bastian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecbfd8da-f383-5c35-91d2-f252d4017d93', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecbfd8da-f383-5c35-91d2-f252d4017d93'}, {'abstract': 'We are retreating to our tribal, ethnic and primitively prejudicial quarters. That is President Trump’s legacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/27/opinion/the-national-crackup.html', 'snippet': 'We are retreating to our tribal, ethnic and primitively prejudicial quarters. That is President Trump’s legacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I never cared for the “melting pot” metaphor, in part because it treats a nation of immigrants like a stew with all the cultures cooked out of it. Nor was I a fan of “gorgeous mosaic,” which sounds fine coming from a kindergarten teacher but is flat as a political rallying cry.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/27/opinion/27egan1/27egan1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The National Crackup', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Narrative Shattered by Our National Crackup', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-27T08:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c6d6b51-71cd-5c6e-a002-a793c759fc2c', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c6d6b51-71cd-5c6e-a002-a793c759fc2c'}, {'abstract': 'Fifty years after Kavan’s novel was first published, Lethem revisits her lucid yet anguished work, and her troubled life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/27/books/review/jonathan-lethem-anna-kavan-ice.html', 'snippet': 'Fifty years after Kavan’s novel was first published, Lethem revisits her lucid yet anguished work, and her troubled life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anna Kavan’s “Ice” is a book like the moon is the moon. There’s only one. It’s cold and white, and it stares back, both defiant and impassive, static and frantically on the move, marked by phases, out of reach. It may even seem to be following you. It is a book that hides, and glints, like “the girl” who is at the center of its stark, fable-like tableau of catastrophe, pursuit and repetition-compulsion. The tale might seem simple: a desperate love triangle played out in a world jarred into ecocatastrophe by political and scientific crimes. The narrator, whose resolute search for the girl might appear at first benign or even heroic, nonetheless slowly converges with the personality and motives of the sadistic, controlling “warden,” who is the book’s antagonist and the narrator’s double. Though “Ice” is always lucid and direct, nothing in it is simple, and it gathers to itself the properties of both a labyrinth and a mirror.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/29/books/review/29Lethem/29Lethem-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 869, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/29/books/review/29Lethem/29Lethem-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 869}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/29/books/review/29Lethem/29Lethem-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 708, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'