inal': 'By Scott Cacciola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e66fcc7-99d9-5a77-9e12-78d97091fb5e', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e66fcc7-99d9-5a77-9e12-78d97091fb5e'}, {'abstract': 'I suspected my wife wasn’t going to join me and my son for Halloween because, once again, she was going psychotic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/27/well/family/when-the-make-believe-world-is-all-too-real.html', 'snippet': 'I suspected my wife wasn’t going to join me and my son for Halloween because, once again, she was going psychotic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For my son Jonas’s first Halloween, when he was 5 months old, I dressed the two of us as matching lumberjacks. For the second, we were characters from the movie “Up.” I was Carl, the old man, my wife was Ellie, and Jonas was Russell, the enthusiastic Wilderness Explorer. We tied a dozen balloons to our bulldog’s collar, to make him the house. In our version, the wife didn’t die at the beginning of the movie, and we all lived happily ever after.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/31/well/family/well-ties-halloween/well-ties-halloween-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 519, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/31/well/family/well-ties-halloween/well-ties-halloween-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 519}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/31/well/family/well-ties-halloween/well-ties-halloween-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 886, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '