: None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a194c180-5fa5-5750-877a-b7ad4fb86da9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a194c180-5fa5-5750-877a-b7ad4fb86da9'}, {'abstract': 'Steven J. Ross discusses his new history of Nazi activities in Hollywood, and the spy ring that helped to foil fascist plans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/29/books/review/tell-us-5-things-about-your-book-hitler-in-los-angeles.html', 'snippet': 'Steven J. Ross discusses his new history of Nazi activities in Hollywood, and the spy ring that helped to foil fascist plans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adolf Hitler and Joseph Goebbels “saw Hollywood as central to their efforts to win over the American public and the world to their cause,” Steven J. Ross writes in his new book, “Hitler in Los Angeles.” Nazis and other fascists in L.A. planned terrorist attacks against Jews and also plotted to kill high-profile Hollywood figures including, according to Mr. Ross’s account, Al Jolson, Charlie Chaplin and Samuel Goldwyn. Leon Lewis, one of the founding members of the Anti-Defamation League and a veteran of World War I, recruited fellow military veterans and others to create a spy ring to foil the Nazis’ plans and counter their influence. Below, Mr. Ross, a professor of history at the University of Southern California, discusses the initial discoveries that led him to dive into the subject, the surprising things he learned about a German previously known as an arch-villain and more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/30/arts/30ross-fivequestions1/23ross-fivequestions1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/30/arts/30ross-fivequestions1/23ross-fivequestions1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheig