nk': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Halloween', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-30T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Family', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe395ca-cd97-543e-990d-836c6c4e43d2', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe395ca-cd97-543e-990d-836c6c4e43d2'}, {'abstract': 'Responding to a child’s anger with adult anger makes things worse, and misses the question of what is going on with the child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/30/well/family/managing-the-storm-of-a-toddlers-tantrum.html', 'snippet': 'Responding to a child’s anger with adult anger makes things worse, and misses the question of what is going on with the child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pity the poor parent whose child is throwing a public tantrum. No, don’t come up with a list of excellent parenting strategies that you’re quite sure would have averted this unhappy scene. And don’t use it as an opportunity to deliver a little homily about kids today or parents today and how much better things used to be. Just pity the poor parent, offer to help if you think you can, and otherwise, give everyone some space.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/23/well/family/well-family-tantrum/well-family-tantrum-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/23/well/family/well-fam