 'Vegan with Latin American influences in the East Village, a Korean steakhouse in NoMad, and other restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/31/dining/pasta-flyer-nyc-restaurant-openings.html', 'snippet': 'Vegan with Latin American influences in the East Village, a Korean steakhouse in NoMad, and other restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PASTA FLYER When you promise miracles like a good plate of pasta in mere minutes, you have to get it right. That effort is what has taken the chef, Mark Ladner, many more months than he anticipated to engineer his concept. Now, with skills honed in the serious precincts of Del Posto, after time in some of the earthier Batali-Bastianich properties like Otto, he’s ready. Enter the restaurant up some stairs, and you go straight to the counter where the action is. “It’s a completely new design, and it’s much better,” he said. Last spring, poised to open, he and his business partner, Nastassia Lopez, decided their kitchen plan wasn’t working, so they had the whole thing rebuilt to make the pasta assembly line more efficient, always with an eye on the stopwatch. Mr. Ladner’s combination of speed and quality at a low price involves a freezing method. He offers three types of pastas with a basic marinara sauce for $6 to $7; adding pesto or meat costs another dollar, and 50 cents gets you a topping like cheese. Beverages include wine. To one side in the high-ceilinged space are plush banquettes, tables and chairs: 510 Avenue of the Americas (13th Street), no phone, pastaflyer.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/11/01/dining/01Off/01Off-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/11/01/dining/01Off/01Off-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 