ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hero Dog: Hilde Cracks the Case #1 (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-10-31T17:06:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Concepción De León', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Concepción', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'De León', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d118936-f56f-5bfd-970b-1907610e4920', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d118936-f56f-5bfd-970b-1907610e4920'}, {'abstract': '180 the Store showcases brands represented by the public relations firm Williamson, which happens to live upstairs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/31/fashion/mens-style/a-tribeca-store-with-built-in-pr-buzz.html', 'snippet': '180 the Store showcases brands represented by the public relations firm Williamson, which happens to live upstairs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s no surprise that fashion boutiques hire publicists, but how many publicists open their own boutique? That’s the back story to 180 the Store in TriBeCa, which was opened by Denise Williamson in the same building, 180 Duane Street, where she runs her fashion P.R. agency, Williamson PR. The idea is to showcase her clients (mostly Japanese labels including Visvim and Nanamica), as well as other brands that she adores. The 2,500-square-foot space is constantly in flux, and is used regularly for events and pop-up installations.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/11/03/fashion/03ms-FOURQUESTIONS/03ms-FOURQUESTIONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/11/03/fashion/03ms-FOURQUESTIONS/03ms-FOURQUESTIO